body:
The undo/redo tests only verify that UI buttons are rendered and disabled states are correct, but don't test the actual undo/redo behavior. Consider adding tests that:
1. Add a component
2. Click undo
3. Verify the component is removed
4. Click redo
5. Verify the component is restored

This would catch the critical bug where undo/redo doesn't actually update the displayed components.
```suggestion
    });

    it('should undo and redo component changes on the canvas', () => {
      const onChange = vi.fn();
      const { container } = render(<PageCanvasEditor schema={EMPTY_PAGE} onChange={onChange} />);

      // 1. Add a component to the canvas
      const addComponentButton = screen.getByTestId('canvas-add-component');
      fireEvent.click(addComponentButton);

      // 2. Verify the component is rendered on the canvas
      expect(container.querySelector('[data-testid="canvas-component"]')).not.toBeNull();

      // 3. Click undo and verify the component is removed
      fireEvent.click(screen.getByTestId('canvas-undo'));
      expect(container.querySelector('[data-testid="canvas-component"]')).toBeNull();

      // 4. Click redo and verify the component is restored
      fireEvent.click(screen.getByTestId('canvas-redo'));
      expect(container.querySelector('[data-testid="canvas-component"]')).not.toBeNull();
    });
```

The undo/redo tests only verify that UI buttons are rendered and disabled states are correct, but don't test the actual undo/redo behavior. Consider adding tests that:
1. Add a widget
2. Click undo
3. Verify the widget is removed
4. Click redo
5. Verify the widget is restored

This would provide better coverage of the undo/redo functionality.
```suggestion
    });

    it('should undo and redo widget addition', () => {
      const onChange = vi.fn();
      render(<DashboardEditor schema={EMPTY_DASHBOARD} onChange={onChange} />);

      // Add a widget
      fireEvent.click(screen.getByTestId('dashboard-add-metric'));
      expect(onChange).toHaveBeenCalled();
      const afterAdd = onChange.mock.calls[onChange.mock.calls.length - 1][0] as DashboardSchema;
      expect(afterAdd.widgets).toHaveLength(1);

      // Undo the addition
      fireEvent.click(screen.getByTestId('dashboard-undo'));
      const afterUndo = onChange.mock.calls[onChange.mock.calls.length - 1][0] as DashboardSchema;
      expect(afterUndo.widgets).toHaveLength(0);

      // Redo the addition
      fireEvent.click(screen.getByTestId('dashboard-redo'));
      const afterRedo = onChange.mock.calls[onChange.mock.calls.length - 1][0] as DashboardSchema;
      expect(afterRedo.widgets).toHaveLength(1);
    });
```

When undo or redo is triggered via keyboard shortcuts, the internal `currentSchema` state updates but the parent component's `onChange` callback is not invoked. This means the parent component won't be aware of the undo/redo operation and won't update its own state accordingly.

Consider adding a useEffect that watches for changes to `currentSchema` and calls `onChange` when it changes due to undo/redo operations. Alternatively, wrap the undo/redo functions to call onChange after they execute.
```suggestion

  const lastNotifiedSchemaRef = useRef<DashboardSchema | null>(null);

  // Ensure parent onChange is notified for all schema changes, including undo/redo
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!onChange) return;

    // Skip notifying on initial mount; just record the baseline schema
    if (lastNotifiedSchemaRef.current === null) {
      lastNotifiedSchemaRef.current = currentSchema;
      return;
    }

    if (lastNotifiedSchemaRef.current !== currentSchema) {
      lastNotifiedSchemaRef.current = currentSchema;
      onChange(currentSchema);
    }
  }, [currentSchema, onChange]);
```
